|~ `::map` and what is declared under it: the refusals of a
   payload it cannot read, and the verbs of maps. A map's
   elements are its values, and its keys travel with them: a verb whose
   answer names the map keeps the keys of the entries it keeps and
   answers under the tags of the map, `union` and `merge` under those it
   shares with the other map, and `map`, whose answer is any
   value, answers under none.
   The rules it holds: [QSDSP#residence]. ~|

:map
  |~~ A map holds named values under keywords, `{:id 7 :tags ["new"]}`:
      the value of a JSON object, a record of facts, and the shape an
      answer is written in. A value is read by its key, `/id`, a missing
      key a failure and `query /id` null; `keys` answers the keys as a set,
      and `entries` the pairs `[key value]`.

      It is written in braces, each keyword followed by its value,
      `{:apples 3 :pears 5}`, or as JSON writes it,
      `{"apples": 3, "pears": 5}`, whose keys read as keywords, so JSON
      pastes into a query as it is; one form a literal, and a key written
      twice keeps its last value. `::map{:id 7}` is `{:id 7}`. JSON
      writes a map as an object, and maps rank after sets.

      A map literal is a value its text fixes, and the shape of an
      answer is a map read as the template of `get`, its paths read and
      its quotes run against the subject,
      `{:first "Ann" :age 30} | get {:who /first}` answering
      `{:who "Ann"}`. `map ~(…)` runs a step on each value
      under its key, `filter ~(…)` keeps the entries whose value holds,
      and `toMap` builds a map back from its pairs.
      ::snippet~(::map | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals.
      ::link~(::keyword) ::link~(::path)
      ::link~(::qlang/values)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts
        |~~ A map, read as itself. A value of `::map` already, a map under
            a tag of its own and an error at rest among them, stays as it
            is, `payload` reading the map beneath, and the pairs of a vector,
            refused here, build a map by `toMap`.
            ::link~(::map) ::link~(::vec/toMap)
            ::link~(::any/payload)
        ~~|
        ::map
    | reveals :count
    | reveals :shape
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::map` reads its payload as the map it is, and a value of no
            map is refused: the pairs of a vector build a map by `toMap`,
            `[[:apples 3]] | toMap`.
        ~~|
    | proves :isAMap |~~ Braces make a map. ~~| ~({:id 7} | tag | eq ::map)
    | proves :buildsAMap
        |~~ A map under its own tag is the map. ~~|
        ~(::map{:id 7} | eq {:id 7})
    | proves :readsJson
        |~~ A JSON object is a map literal. ~~|
        ~("{\"id\": 7, \"flags\": [true, null]}" | parse | answer | eq {:id 7 :flags [true null]})
    | proves :keepsTheLastOfAKey
        |~~ A key written twice keeps the last value. ~~|
        ~("{:status :new :status :paid}" | parse | answer | eq {:status :paid})
    | proves :buildsBackFromEntries
        |~~ The entries of a map build it back by `toMap`. ~~|
        ~({:apples 3 :pears 5} | entries | toMap | eq {:apples 3 :pears 5})
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number payload is refused. ~~|
        ~(::map#1 !| get [~(tag) ~(found | tag)] | eq [::map/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesThePairsOfAVector
        |~~ The pairs of a vector are refused; `toMap` builds them into a
            map.
        ~~|
        ~([[:apples 3]] | tag ::map !| get [~(tag) ~(found | tag)] | eq [::map/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::vec])
    | proves :keepsAValueOfItsKind
        |~~ A value of `::map` already, a map under a tag of its own and an
            error at rest, stays as it is; `payload` reads the map beneath.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~(::map::Box{:id 7} | tag) ~(::map!{:id 7} | tag) ~(::Box{:id 7} | payload | tag)] | eq [::Box ::error ::map]);

:map/count
  |~~ Answers how many entries the map holds,
      `{:apples 3 :pears 5} | count` answering `2` and the empty map `0`, a
      map under a tag counting the entries beneath it. `count` takes no
      value: the entries to count are chosen before it,
      `filter ~(gt 1) | count`. A vector and a set answer by the `count`
      of vectors, and a string by a `count` of its own, its characters.
      ::link~(::map/empty) ::link~(::vec/count)
      ::link~(::string/count)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ How many entries the map holds. ~~| ::number
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `count` takes no value; the entries to count are chosen before
            it, `filter ~(gt 1) | count`.
        ~~|
    | proves :countsTheEntries
        |~~ Three entries count three. ~~|
        ~({:apples 3 :pears 5 :plums 2} | count | eq 3)
    | proves :countsNothingInTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map counts no entry. ~~|
        ~({} | count | eq 0)
    | proves :countsBeneathATag
        |~~ A map under a tag counts the entries beneath it. ~~|
        ~(::Basket{:apples 3 :pears 5} | count | eq 2)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAPredicate
        |~~ A predicate given to `count` is refused; `filter` chooses
            first.
        ~~|
        ~({:apples 3 :pears 5} | count ~(gt 4) !| tag | eq ::map/count/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/size
  |~~ The name other languages give `count`: answers how many entries the
      map holds, as `count` does, `{:apples 3 :pears 5} | size` answering
      `2`.
      ::link~(::map/count)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ How many entries the map holds. ~~| ::number
    | proves :answersAsCount
        |~~ `size` answers as `count`. ~~|
        ~({:apples 3 :pears 5} | get [~(size) ~(count)] | eq [2 2])
    | impl ~(count);

:map/length
  |~~ The name other languages give `count`: answers how many entries the
      map holds, as `count` does, `{:apples 3 :pears 5} | length`
      answering `2`.
      ::link~(::map/count)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ How many entries the map holds. ~~| ::number
    | proves :answersAsCount
        |~~ `length` answers as `count`. ~~|
        ~({:apples 3 :pears 5} | get [~(length) ~(count)] | eq [2 2])
    | impl ~(count);

:map/empty
  |~~ Answers whether the map holds no entry, `{} | empty` answering
      `true`; it takes no value. A key held with a null value is an entry,
      so `{:note null} | empty` answers `false`. A vector and a set answer by
      the `empty` of vectors.
      ::link~(::map/count) ::link~(::vec/empty)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the map holds no entry, `false` otherwise. ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :holdsOfTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map is empty. ~~|
        ~({} | empty)
    | proves :failsOfAKeyWithANullValue
        |~~ A key held with a null value is an entry. ~~|
        ~({:note null} | empty | not)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/first
  |~~ Answers the value of the first entry, in the order the map holds its
      entries, the order they were written, or null for the empty map; it
      takes no value. The entry itself, key and value, is
      `entries | first`, while `keys | first` answers the key first by
      name. The first entries of a map are `take 2`. A vector answers its
      first element and a set its least by the `first` of vectors.
      ::link~(::map/last) ::link~(::map/entries)
      ::link~(::vec/first)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The value of the first entry, null for the empty map. ~~|
        ::any
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `first` answers one value; the first entries of a map are
            `take 2`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheFirstValue
        |~~ The value of the first entry answers. ~~|
        ~({:mon 12 :tue 15} | first | eq 12)
    | proves :followsTheOrderWritten
        |~~ The first entry is the first written, whatever its key. ~~|
        ~({:tue 15 :mon 12} | first | eq 15)
    | proves :answersNullForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map answers null. ~~|
        ~({} | first | eq null)
    | proves :readsTheEntryByEntries
        |~~ The first entry, key and value, is read by `entries`. ~~|
        ~({:tue 15 :mon 12} | entries | first | eq [:tue 15])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesACount
        |~~ A count given to `first` is refused; `take` takes it. ~~|
        ~({:mon 12 :tue 15} | first 2 !| tag | eq ::map/first/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/last
  |~~ Answers the value of the last entry, in the order the map holds its
      entries, or null for the empty map; it takes no value. The entry
      itself, key and value, is `entries | last`. A vector answers its
      last element and a set its greatest by the `last` of vectors.
      ::link~(::map/first) ::link~(::vec/last)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The value of the last entry, null for the empty map. ~~|
        ::any
    | proves :answersTheLastValue
        |~~ The value of the last entry answers. ~~|
        ~({:mon 12 :tue 15} | last | eq 15)
    | proves :answersNullForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map answers null. ~~|
        ~({} | last | eq null)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/sum
  |~~ Answers the sum of the values, each a number,
      `{:rent 900 :food 300} | sum` answering `1200` and the empty map `0`;
      it takes no value. A value that is no number is refused, the path of
      its frame naming its key, and a field of each value is read first by
      `map`, `map /amount | sum`. A vector and a set sum their elements by
      the `sum` of vectors, and two fields of one record add by
      `sumOf /price /tax`.
      ::link~(::map/avg) ::link~(::vec/sum)
      ::link~(::any/sumOf)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The sum of the values, `0` for the empty map. ~~| ::number
    | throws :ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `sum` adds the numbers the map holds; the path of its frame
            names the first entry whose value is none, and a field is
            read first, `map /amount | sum`.
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `sum` takes no value: a field of each value is read first by
            `map`, `map /amount | sum`.
        ~~|
    | proves :addsTheValues
        |~~ The sum adds every value. ~~|
        ~({:rent 900 :food 300} | sum | eq 1200)
    | proves :answersZeroForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map sums to zero. ~~|
        ~({} | sum | eq 0)
    | proves :sumsAFieldReadFirst
        |~~ A field of each value is read by `map` before the sum. ~~|
        ~({:rent {:amount 900} :food {:amount 300}} | map /amount | sum | eq 1200)
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string among the values is refused under its key. ~~|
        ~({:rent 900 :food "300"} | sum !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | get /food | tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(subject | expected)]
          |  eq [::map/sum/ElementKindNotTakenError ::string /food #[::number]])
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesANull
        |~~ A null value is no number either. ~~|
        ~({:rent null} | sum !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path)] | eq [::map/sum/ElementKindNotTakenError /rent])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAField
        |~~ A field given to `sum` is refused; `map` reads it first. ~~|
        ~({:rent {:amount 900}} | sum /amount !| tag | eq ::map/sum/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/avg
  |~~ Answers the mean of the values, each a number,
      `{:mon 12 :tue 15} | avg` answering `13.5`, or null for the empty
      map, which has no mean, the sum of the values over their count as
      `avg` of vectors reads it, staying among the finite doubles,
      `{:high 1e308 :low -1e308} | avg` answering `0`; it takes
      no value. A value that is no number is refused, the path of its frame
      naming its key, and a field of each value is read first by `map`,
      `map /amount | avg`. A vector and a set answer by the `avg` of
      vectors.
      ::link~(::map/sum) ::link~(::vec/avg)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The mean of the values, null for the empty map. ~~|
        #[::number ::null]
    | throws :ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `avg` takes the mean of the numbers the map holds; the path
            of its frame names the first entry whose value is none.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheMean
        |~~ The mean of twelve and sixteen is fourteen. ~~|
        ~({:mon 12 :tue 16} | avg | eq 14)
    | proves :answersNullForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map has no mean. ~~|
        ~({} | avg | eq null)
    | proves :averagesTheOppositeEnds
        |~~ The mean of a number near one end of the doubles and its
            negation is zero.
        ~~|
        ~({:high 1e308 :low -1e308} | avg | eq 0)
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string among the values is refused under its key. ~~|
        ~({:mon 12 :tue "15"} | avg !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | get /tue | tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(subject | expected)]
          |  eq [::map/avg/ElementKindNotTakenError ::string /tue #[::number]])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/min
  |~~ Answers the least value in the one order of values, the values of
      one kind, or null for the empty map: `{:tea 4 :cake 6} | min` answers
      `4`. A key the call gives, code run on each value, ranks the values,
      `{:ann {:age 30} :bob {:age 25}} | min /age` answering `{:age 25}`,
      and a key under `desc` ranks in reverse. The entry of the least
      value, key and value, is `entries | min /1`. A vector and a set
      answer by the `min` of vectors.
      ::link~(::map/max) ::link~(::map/sort)
      ::link~(::desc) ::link~(::vec/min)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :key
        |~~ The code that ranks each value, null when the call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::path ::quote]
    | throws :KeyKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key is a path, a quote or null: written `~(get /age)`, it
            runs on each value, while `(get /age)` is computed once at the call,
            against the map, and refused with what it answered.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The least value, null for the empty map. ~~| ::any
    | proves :answersTheLeastValue
        |~~ The least value answers. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake 6} | min | eq 4)
    | proves :relatesValuesOfOneKind
        |~~ A number and a string are no pair a minimum relates. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake "six"} | min !| tag | eq ::call/KindsMismatchedError)
    | proves :ranksByTheKey
        |~~ The key ranks each value. ~~|
        ~({:ann {:age 30} :bob {:age 25}} | min /age | eq {:age 25})
    | proves :ranksInReverseUnderDesc
        |~~ A key under `desc` ranks in reverse. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake 6} | min ~(desc) | eq 6)
    | proves :findsTheKeyByEntries
        |~~ The key of the least value is read by `entries`. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake 6} | entries | min /1 | get /0 | eq :tea)
    | proves :answersNullForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map has no least value. ~~|
        ~({} | min | eq null)
    | proves :KeyKindNotTakenError/refusesAKeyComputedAtTheCall
        |~~ A key written without its quote is computed at the call and
            refused.
        ~~|
        ~({:ann {:age 30}} | min (get /age)
          !| get [~(tag) ~(key | found | tag)]
          |  eq [::map/min/KeyKindNotTakenError ::path/KeyNotInMapError])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/max
  |~~ Answers the greatest value in the one order of values, the values
      of one kind, or null for the empty map; a key the call gives
      ranks the values, as for `min`. The entry of the greatest
      value, key and value, is `entries | max /1`. A vector and a set
      answer by the `max` of vectors.
      ::link~(::map/min) ::link~(::vec/max)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :key
        |~~ The code that ranks each value, null when the call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::path ::quote]
    | throws :KeyKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key is a path, a quote or null, `max /age`. ~~|
    | returns |~~ The greatest value, null for the empty map. ~~| ::any
    | proves :answersTheGreatestValue
        |~~ The greatest value answers. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake 6} | max | eq 6)
    | proves :relatesValuesOfOneKind
        |~~ A number and a string are no pair a maximum relates. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake "six"} | max !| tag | eq ::call/KindsMismatchedError)
    | proves :findsTheKeyByEntries
        |~~ The key of the greatest value is read by `entries`. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake 6} | entries | max /1 | get /0 | eq :cake)
    | proves :answersNullForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map has no greatest value. ~~|
        ~({} | max | eq null)
    | proves :KeyKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A key that is no quote is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4} | max 1 !| tag | eq ::map/max/KeyKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :ranksByTheKey
        |~~ The key the call gives ranks each value. ~~|
        ~({:ann {:age 30} :bob {:age 40}} | max /age | eq {:age 40})
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/map
  |~~ Answers the map of what the code answers for each value, under the
      same keys in their order, `{:tea 4 :cake 6} | map ~(add 1)` answering
      `{:tea 5 :cake 7}`. The code is a quote run on each value as its
      subject, not on its entry, so a key is read beside its value by
      `entries | map ~(…)`, and `toMap` builds the map back from the pairs.
      The first error the code answers for a value is the answer of the
      call, and the answers stand in a map of their own, under no tag of
      the subject. A vector and a set answer `map` by the verb of vectors.
      ::link~(::map/filter) ::link~(::map/entries)
      ::link~(::vec/map)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :code
        |~~ The code run on each value, a quote. ~~|
        /0 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :CodeKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The code is a quote, `map ~(add 1)`, or a path, `map /name`;
            written without the tilde, `map (add 1)`, it is computed once
            at the call, against the map, and refused with what it
            answered.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ What the code answered for each value, under the same keys, a
            map under no tag.
        ~~|
        ::any
    | proves :keepsTheKeys
        |~~ Each value is replaced under its key. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake 6} | map ~(add 1) | eq {:tea 5 :cake 7})
    | proves :runsOnTheValue
        |~~ The code runs on the value, not on its entry. ~~|
        ~({:ann ["tea" "cake" "jam"]} | map ~count | eq {:ann 3})
    | proves :readsAKeyThroughEntries
        |~~ A key is read beside its value through the entries. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake 6} | entries | map /0 | eq [:tea :cake])
    | proves :answersTheFirstFailure
        |~~ The first error, in the order of the keys, answers the call. ~~|
        ~({:tea "four" :cake "six"} | map ~(add 1) !| trace | first | subject | eq "four")
    | proves :answersUnderNoTag
        |~~ The answers stand under no tag of the subject. ~~|
        ~(::Prices{:tea 4} | map ~(add 1) | eq {:tea 5})
    | proves :CodeKindNotTakenError/refusesAStepComputedAtTheCall
        |~~ A step written without its quote is refused. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4} | map (add 1) !| tag | eq ::map/map/CodeKindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/filter
  |~~ Answers the entries whose value the predicate answers true for,
      their keys kept, under the tags of the map:
      `{:tea 4 :cake 6 :jam 3} | filter ~(gt 3)` answers
      `{:tea 4 :cake 6}`. The predicate is a quote the call gives, run on
      each value and not on its entry, so a test of a key reads the
      entries and builds the map back,
      `entries | filter ~(get /0 | ne :tmp) | toMap`, and a key leaves by
      `minus`. The predicate answers a boolean; any other answer is
      refused with the key of its entry. A vector and a set keep their
      elements by the `filter` of vectors.
      ::link~(::map/every) ::link~(::map/minus)
      ::link~(::map/entries) ::link~(::vec/filter)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :predicate
        |~~ The code each value is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
        /0 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :PredicateKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The predicate is a quote, `filter ~(gt 1)`, or a path,
            `filter /active`; written without the tilde, `filter (gt 1)`,
            it is computed once at the call and refused.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The entries whose values the predicate accepted, under the
            tags of the map.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | throws :PredicateAnswerKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The predicate of `filter` answers a boolean for the value of
            each entry; the path of its frame names the entry whose
            predicate answered another kind.
        ~~|
    | proves :keepsTheKeys
        |~~ The entries kept keep their keys. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake 6 :jam 3} | filter ~(gt 3) | eq {:tea 4 :cake 6})
    | proves :keepsNothingOfTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map filters to itself. ~~|
        ~({} | filter ~(gt 0) | eq {})
    | proves :answersUnderTheTagOfTheMap
        |~~ A map under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Prices{:tea 4 :jam 3} | filter ~(gt 3) | eq ::Prices{:tea 4})
    | proves :runsOnTheValue
        |~~ The predicate meets the value, not its entry: a number has no
            parts.
        ~~|
        ~({:tea 4} | filter /0 !| tag | eq ::path/SubjectHasNoPartsError)
    | proves :PredicateKindNotTakenError/refusesAPredicateComputedAtTheCall
        |~~ A predicate written without its quote is refused. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4} | filter (gt 3) !| tag | eq ::map/filter/PredicateKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :PredicateAnswerKindNotTakenError/refusesANumberAnswer
        |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused under the key. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4} | filter ~(sub 3) !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(predicate | answer | tag) ~(predicate | expected)]
          |  eq [::map/filter/PredicateAnswerKindNotTakenError /tea ::number #[::boolean]])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/every
  |~~ Answers whether the predicate answers true for every value,
      `{:tea 4 :cake 6} | every ~(gt 0)` answering `true`, and `true` for the
      empty map, which holds no value to fail it. The predicate is a quote
      the call gives, run on each value, and answers a boolean; any other
      answer is refused with the key of its entry. A vector and a set ask
      their elements by the `every` of vectors.
      ::link~(::map/any) ::link~(::map/filter)
      ::link~(::vec/every)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :predicate
        |~~ The code each value is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
        /0 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :PredicateKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The predicate is a quote, `every ~(gt 0)`, or a path,
            `every /active`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the predicate held for every value, `false`
            otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | throws :PredicateAnswerKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The predicate of `every` answers a boolean for the value of
            each entry; the path of its frame names the entry whose
            predicate answered another kind.
        ~~|
    | proves :holdsOfEveryValue
        |~~ Every value is positive. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake 6 :jam 3} | every ~(gt 0))
    | proves :asksTheValues
        |~~ A map is asked of its values, and one that fails fails
            `every`.
        ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake 0 :jam 3} | every ~(gt 0) | not)
    | proves :holdsOfTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map holds no value to fail. ~~|
        ~({} | every ~(gt 0))
    | proves :PredicateAnswerKindNotTakenError/refusesANumberAnswer
        |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused under the key. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4} | every ~(sub 3) !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(predicate | answer | tag) ~(predicate | expected)]
          |  eq [::map/every/PredicateAnswerKindNotTakenError /tea ::number #[::boolean]])
    | proves :PredicateKindNotTakenError/refusesCodeComputedAtTheCall
        |~~ A predicate written without its quote is computed at the call
            and refused.
        ~~|
        ~({:tea 4} | every (gt 0) !| tag | eq ::map/every/PredicateKindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/any
  |~~ Answers whether the predicate answers true for some value,
      `{:tea 0 :cake 2} | any ~(gt 0)` answering `true`, and `false` for the
      empty map. The predicate is a quote the call gives, run on each
      value, and answers a boolean; any other answer is refused with the
      key of its entry. A vector and a set ask their elements by the `any`
      of vectors.
      ::link~(::map/every) ::link~(::map/filter)
      ::link~(::vec/any)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :predicate
        |~~ The code each value is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
        /0 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :PredicateKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The predicate is a quote, `any ~(gt 0)`, or a path,
            `any /vip`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the predicate held for some value, `false`
            otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | throws :PredicateAnswerKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The predicate of `any` answers a boolean for the value of each
            entry; the path of its frame names the entry whose predicate
            answered another kind.
        ~~|
    | proves :holdsOfSomeValue
        |~~ One positive value is enough. ~~|
        ~({:tea 0 :cake 0 :jam 2} | any ~(gt 0))
    | proves :failsOfTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map holds no value to pass. ~~|
        ~({} | any ~(gt 0) | not)
    | proves :PredicateAnswerKindNotTakenError/refusesANumberAnswer
        |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused under the key. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4} | any ~(sub 3) !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(predicate | answer | tag) ~(predicate | expected)]
          |  eq [::map/any/PredicateAnswerKindNotTakenError /tea ::number #[::boolean]])
    | proves :PredicateKindNotTakenError/refusesCodeComputedAtTheCall
        |~~ A predicate written without its quote is computed at the call
            and refused.
        ~~|
        ~({:tea 4} | any (gt 0) !| tag | eq ::map/any/PredicateKindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/sort
  |~~ Answers the entries ordered by their values in the one order, their
      keys kept, under the tags of the map: `{:cake 6 :tea 4} | sort`
      answers `{:tea 4 :cake 6}`. A key the call gives, code run on each
      value, ranks them, `sort /age`, and a key under `desc` descends,
      `sort ~(desc)`. The entries of the answer read in order by `entries`
      and `vals`, while `keys` answers its keys by name. A vector and a
      set order by a `sort` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/min) ::link~(::desc)
      ::link~(::vec/sort)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :key
        |~~ The code that ranks each value, null when the call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::path ::quote]
    | throws :KeyKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key is a path, a quote or null, `sort /age`. ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The entries in the order the key ranks them, under the tags of
            the map.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | proves :ordersByValue
        |~~ The entries order by their values. ~~|
        ~({:cake 6 :tea 4 :jam 3} | sort | vals | eq [3 4 6])
    | proves :ordersByTheKey
        |~~ The key ranks each value, and each value keeps its key. ~~|
        ~({:ann {:age 30} :bob {:age 25}} | sort /age | entries | map /0 | eq [:bob :ann])
    | proves :descendsUnderDesc
        |~~ A key under `desc` descends. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake 6} | sort ~(desc) | vals | eq [6 4])
    | proves :answersUnderTheTagOfTheMap
        |~~ A map under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Prices{:cake 6 :tea 4} | sort | eq ::Prices{:tea 4 :cake 6})
    | proves :KeyKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A key that is no code is refused. ~~|
        ~({:tea 4 :cake 6} | sort 1 !| tag | eq ::map/sort/KeyKindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/take
  |~~ Answers the first entries of the map, as many as the count the call
      gives, in the order the map holds them, under the tags of the map:
      `{:ann 41 :bob 43 :eve 47} | take 2` answers `{:ann 41 :bob 43}`.
      A count past the size takes the whole map and a count below one
      takes none. The count is a whole number. A vector and a set take by
      the `take` of vectors, and a string by a `take` of its own.
      ::link~(::map/drop) ::link~(::map/first)
      ::link~(::vec/take)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :count |~~ How many entries, a whole number. ~~| /0 ::number
    | throws :CountKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The count is a number, `take 2`; text that holds one is read
            by `toNumber` first, `take ("2" | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | holds :CountNotIntegerError
        |~~ The count is a whole number, `take 2`; a fraction is rounded
            first, `take (1.5 | round)`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | eq (floor))
    | returns |~~ The first entries, under the tags of the map. ~~| ::map
    | proves :takesTheFirstEntries
        |~~ Two entries of a map are its first two. ~~|
        ~({:ann 41 :bob 43 :eve 47} | take 2 | eq {:ann 41 :bob 43})
    | proves :takesTheWholeMapForACountPastItsSize
        |~~ A count past the size takes every entry. ~~|
        ~({:ann 41} | take 99 | eq {:ann 41})
    | proves :takesNothingForACountBelowOne
        |~~ A count below one takes no entry. ~~|
        ~({:ann 41} | take -1 | eq {})
    | proves :answersUnderTheTagOfTheMap
        |~~ A map under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Race{:ann 41 :bob 43} | take 1 | eq ::Race{:ann 41})
    | proves :CountNotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
        |~~ A fraction is no count. ~~|
        ~({:ann 41} | take 1.5 !| tag | eq ::map/take/CountNotIntegerError)
    | proves :CountKindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A count written as a string is refused. ~~|
        ~({:ann 41} | take "1" !| tag | eq ::map/take/CountKindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/drop
  |~~ Answers the entries after the first ones, as many as the count the
      call gives, in the order the map holds them, under the tags of the
      map: `{:ann 41 :bob 43 :eve 47} | drop 2` answers `{:eve 47}`. A
      count below one drops none and a count past the size drops every
      entry. The count is a whole number. A key leaves a map by `minus`,
      which takes the keys to leave out, `minus #[:bob]`. A vector and a
      set drop by the `drop` of vectors, and a string by a `drop` of its
      own.
      ::link~(::map/take) ::link~(::map/minus)
      ::link~(::vec/drop)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :count |~~ How many entries, a whole number. ~~| /0 ::number
    | throws :CountKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The count is a number, `drop 2`; text that holds one is read
            by `toNumber` first, `drop ("2" | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | holds :CountNotIntegerError
        |~~ The count is a whole number, `drop 2`; a fraction is rounded
            first, `drop (1.5 | round)`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | eq (floor))
    | returns
        |~~ The entries after the ones dropped, under the tags of the map.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | proves :dropsTheFirstEntries
        |~~ Dropping two leaves the last. ~~|
        ~({:ann 41 :bob 43 :eve 47} | drop 2 | eq {:eve 47})
    | proves :dropsNothingForACountBelowOne
        |~~ A count below one drops no entry. ~~|
        ~({:ann 41} | drop -1 | eq {:ann 41})
    | proves :dropsEveryEntryForACountPastTheSize
        |~~ A count past the size drops every entry. ~~|
        ~({:ann 41} | drop 5 | eq {})
    | proves :CountKindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A count written as a string is refused. ~~|
        ~({:ann 41} | drop "1" !| tag | eq ::map/drop/CountKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :CountNotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
        |~~ A count that is no whole number is refused. ~~|
        ~({:ann 41} | drop 1.5 !| tag | eq ::map/drop/CountNotIntegerError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/reverse
  |~~ Answers the entries in the reverse order, their keys kept, under the
      tags of the map: `{:ann 41 :bob 43} | reverse` answers
      `{:bob 43 :ann 41}`. It takes no value. A vector and a set reverse by
      a `reverse` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/sort) ::link~(::vec/reverse)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The entries in reverse order, under the tags of the map. ~~|
        ::map
    | proves :reversesTheEntries
        |~~ The entries come in reverse order. ~~|
        ~({:ann 41 :bob 43} | reverse | vals | eq [43 41])
    | proves :keepsTheKeys
        |~~ Each value keeps its key. ~~|
        ~({:ann 41 :bob 43} | reverse | entries | first | eq [:bob 43])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/reduce
  |~~ Answers the seed folded over the values by the reducer, in the order
      the map holds them: `{:rent 900 :food 300} | reduce 0 ~(add)`
      answers `1200`, and the empty map answers the seed. The reducer is
      code: a quote of one word, the name of a verb or its address, names
      the verb the fold calls with the value so far as its subject and
      each value as its first arg, and any other code the fold runs on the
      pair of the value so far and the value, as a vector's fold does. A
      vector and a set fold by the `reduce` of vectors.
      ::link~(::map/sum) ::link~(::vec/reduce)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :seed |~~ The value the fold starts from. ~~| /0
    | takes :reducer
        |~~ Code: a quote of one word, which names the verb the fold calls
            with a value, or a path or a quote it runs on the pair of the
            value so far and the value.
        ~~|
        /1 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :ReducerKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The reducer is a path or a quote, `reduce 0 ~(add)`, after the
            seed: written first, `reduce ~(add) 0`, it is read as the
            seed and the seed as the reducer, and written
            without the tilde, `(add)`, it is computed once at the call;
            either is refused.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The value the fold reached, the seed for the empty map. ~~|
        ::any
    | proves :foldsTheValues
        |~~ The fold adds the values to the seed. ~~|
        ~({:rent 900 :food 300} | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 1200)
    | proves :answersTheSeedForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map answers the seed. ~~|
        ~({} | reduce 7 ~(add) | eq 7)
    | proves :foldsInTheOrderOfTheEntries
        |~~ The fold meets the values in the order the map holds them. ~~|
        ~({:stem "run" :ending "ning"} | reduce "" ~(append) | eq "running")
    | proves :ReducerKindNotTakenError/refusesAReducerComputedAtTheCall
        |~~ A reducer written without its quote is refused. ~~|
        ~({:rent 900} | reduce 0 (add) !| tag | eq ::map/reduce/ReducerKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :ReducerKindNotTakenError/refusesTheReducerFirst
        |~~ A reducer written before the seed is read as the seed, and
            the seed as the reducer.
        ~~|
        ~({:rent 900} | reduce ~(add) 0
          !| get [~(tag) ~(reducer | found | tag)]
          |  eq [::map/reduce/ReducerKindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :runsCodeOnThePair
        |~~ Code of more than one word runs on the pair of the value so
            far and the value.
        ~~|
        ~({:rent 900 :food 300} | reduce 0 ~(sumOf /0 /1) | eq 1200)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/keys
  |~~ Answers the vector of the keys, each a keyword, in the order the map
      holds its entries, as `vals`, `entries` and its print read it:
      `{:name "Ann" :age 30} | keys` answers `[:name :age]`, so the keys
      and the values of one map pair. It takes no value. A key written in
      JSON or read by `parseJson` is a keyword too,
      `{"first name": "Ann"} | keys` answering `[:"first name"]`, and a map
      under a tag answers the keys beneath it. The keys come with their
      values by `entries`, one key is asked for by `has`, and the set of
      them, in the one order, is `keys | toSet`.
      ::link~(::map/vals) ::link~(::map/entries)
      ::link~(::map/has) ::link~(::vec/toSet)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The keys of the map, in the order of its entries. ~~|
        ::vec
    | throws :SubjectNotMapError
        |~~ `keys` reads a map: a vector of maps answers its keys element
            by element, `map ~keys`, and a vector reads its elements by
            their indices, `/0`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheKeysInTheOrderOfTheEntries
        |~~ The keys follow the entries. ~~|
        ~({:name "Alice" :age 30} | keys | eq [:name :age])
    | proves :pairsWithTheValues
        |~~ The keys and the values of one map pair. ~~|
        ~({:b 1 :a 2} | get [~(keys) ~(vals)] | zip | eq [[:b 1] [:a 2]])
    | proves :answersTheSetByToSet
        |~~ The set of the keys, in the one order, is `toSet`. ~~|
        ~({:name "Alice" :age 30} | keys | toSet | eq #[:age :name])
    | proves :readsAKeyWrittenInJson
        |~~ A key written in JSON is a keyword. ~~|
        ~({"first name": "Ann"} | keys | eq [:"first name"])
    | proves :answersNoKeyForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map has no keys. ~~|
        ~({} | keys | eq [])
    | proves :readsTheKeysOfEachMapByMap
        |~~ A vector of maps answers their keys by `map`. ~~|
        ~([{:name "Ann"} {:age 30}] | map ~keys | eq [[:name] [:age]])
    | proves :SubjectNotMapError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number has no keys. ~~|
        ~(42 | keys !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | tag)] | eq [::map/keys/SubjectNotMapError ::number])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/vals
  |~~ Answers the vector of the values, in the order of the entries:
      `{:name "Ann" :age 30} | vals` answers `["Ann" 30]`. It takes no
      value, and a map under a tag answers the values beneath it. The
      values with their keys are `entries`.
      ::link~(::map/keys) ::link~(::map/entries)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The values of the map, in the order of the entries. ~~|
        ::vec
    | throws :SubjectNotMapError
        |~~ `vals` reads a map: a vector is its own values, and a vector
            of maps answers their values element by element, `map ~vals`.
        ~~|
    | proves :keepsTheOrderOfTheEntries
        |~~ The values come in the order of the entries. ~~|
        ~({:name "Alice" :age 30} | vals | eq ["Alice" 30])
    | proves :answersTheEmptyVectorForTheEmptyMap
        |~~ The empty map has no values. ~~|
        ~({} | vals | eq [])
    | proves :SubjectNotMapError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector has no values by key. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | vals !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | tag)] | eq [::map/vals/SubjectNotMapError ::vec])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/entries
  |~~ Answers the vector of the pairs `[key value]` of the map, in the
      order of the entries, each key a keyword: the map with its keys as
      data, for a step that reads a key beside its value, as
      `get [~(tag) ~(payload)]` reads a tagged value. `toMap` builds the map back,
      so a test of a key is
      `entries | filter ~(get /0 | ne :tmp) | toMap`, and a key is spelled
      with its value by turning it to text first,
      `map ~(get [~(get /0 | toString) "@" /1] | join "")`. It takes no
      value, and the empty map answers `[]`.
      ::link~(::map/keys) ::link~(::vec/toMap)
      ::link~(::any/payload)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The pairs of key and value, in the order of the entries. ~~|
        ::vec
    | throws :SubjectNotMapError
        |~~ `entries` reads a map: a vector of maps answers their pairs
            element by element, `map ~entries`.
        ~~|
    | proves :pairsEachKeyWithItsValue
        |~~ Each entry is a pair of its key and its value. ~~|
        ~({:auth {:port 3000} :db {:port 5432}} | entries | first | eq [:auth {:port 3000}])
    | proves :spellsTheKeyWithTheValue
        |~~ The key and its value spell one string. ~~|
        ~({:axios "^1.6.0"} | entries | map ~(get [~(get /0 | toString) "@" /1] | join "")
          | eq ["axios@^1.6.0"])
    | proves :filtersByKeyAndBuildsBack
        |~~ Entries filtered by key build back into a map. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann" :tmp 2} | entries | filter ~(get /0 | ne :tmp) | toMap | eq {:name "Ann"})
    | proves :SubjectNotMapError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector has no entries. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | entries !| get [~(tag) ~(subject | found | tag)] | eq [::map/entries/SubjectNotMapError ::vec])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/has
  |~~ Answers whether the map holds the key the call gives, a keyword, a
      string, a number or a path, each naming the key as `get` reads it, a
      number by its text: `{:name "Ann"} | has :name` answers `true`. A key
      held with a null value is held, `{:phone null} | has :phone`
      answering `true`, so `has` tells a null value from a key the map
      lacks, where `query` answers null for both. The key is computed
      against the subject, so it is written as the keyword that names it,
      `has :name`. A vector and a set ask for an element by the `has` of
      vectors, and a string asks for a part by `contains`.
      ::link~(::any/query) ::link~(::vec/has)
      ::link~(::string/contains)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :key
        |~~ The key the call gives, a keyword, a number, a path or a
            string, computed against the subject.
        ~~|
        /0 #[::keyword ::number ::path ::string]
    | throws :KeyKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key is a keyword or a string, written as the keyword that
            names it, `has :name`, a number, which names the key of its
            text as `get` reads it, or a path, which asks whether it reads,
            `has /address/city`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the map holds the key, `false` otherwise. ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :findsAKeyword
        |~~ A key held is found. ~~|
        ~({:name "Alice"} | has :name)
    | proves :findsAString
        |~~ A string names the key as a keyword does. ~~|
        ~({:name "Alice"} | has "name")
    | proves :findsNoKeyTheMapLacks
        |~~ A key the map lacks is not held. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann"} | has :age | not)
    | proves :holdsAKeyWithANullValue
        |~~ A key held with a null value is held. ~~|
        ~({:phone null} | has :phone)
    | proves :readsNoPathThroughAValueWithoutParts
        |~~ A path through a value that has no parts does not read. ~~|
        ~({:age 30} | has /age/years | not)
    | proves :findsTheKeyOfANumber
        |~~ A number names the key of its text, as `get` reads it. ~~|
        ~({:"1" 5} | has 1)
    | proves :KeyKindNotTakenError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector key is refused. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann"} | has [:name]
          !| get [~(tag) ~(key | found | tag)]
          |  eq [::map/has/KeyKindNotTakenError ::vec])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/union
  |~~ Answers the entries of the subject together with those of the map
      the call gives, which wins a key both hold, under the tags the two
      share: `{:theme :dark :font 12} | union {:font 14 :lang :en}` answers
      `{:theme :dark :font 14 :lang :en}`. The values are not merged
      deeper; a key both hold takes the value of the other map whole. A
      set or a vector of keys unites with no map; a set and a vector unite
      by a `union` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/minus) ::link~(::map/inter)
      ::link~(::set/union)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :other |~~ The other map. ~~| /0 ::map
    | throws :OtherKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The subject unites with a map, `union {:font 14}`: a key comes
            with its value, and a set or a vector of keys alone is
            refused.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The entries of both maps, the other winning a key both hold,
            under the tags they share.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | proves :letsTheOtherWin
        |~~ The other map's value wins a key both hold. ~~|
        ~({:theme :dark :font 12} | union {:font 14 :lang :en} | eq {:theme :dark :font 14 :lang :en})
    | proves :mergesTwoMaps
        |~~ The entries of both are merged. ~~|
        ~({:theme :dark} | union {:lang :en} | eq {:theme :dark :lang :en})
    | proves :takesTheOtherValueWhole
        |~~ A key both hold takes the other's value whole, not merged
            deeper.
        ~~|
        ~({:font {:size 12}} | union {:font {:face "Mono"}} | eq {:font {:face "Mono"}})
    | proves :answersUnderTheTagOfTheSubject
        |~~ A map under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Settings{:theme :dark} | union {:lang :en} | eq ::Settings{:theme :dark :lang :en})
    | proves :OtherKindNotTakenError/refusesASetOfKeys
        |~~ A set of keys unites with no map. ~~|
        ~({:theme :dark} | union #[:lang] !| tag | eq ::map/union/OtherKindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/merge
  |~~ The name other languages give `union` over maps: answers the entries
      of the subject together with those of the map the call gives, which
      wins a key both hold, as `union` does,
      `{:theme :dark :font 12} | merge {:font 14}` answering
      `{:theme :dark :font 14}`; the values are not merged deeper.
      ::link~(::map/union)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :other |~~ The other map. ~~| /0 ::map
    | throws :OtherKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The subject merges with a map, `merge {:font 14}`: a key comes with
            its value.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The entries of both maps, the other winning a key both hold,
            under the tags they share.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | proves :answersAsUnion
        |~~ `merge` answers as `union`, the other map winning a key both
            hold.
        ~~|
        ~({:theme :dark :font 12} | get [~(merge {:font 14}) ~(union {:font 14})] | eq [{:theme :dark :font 14} {:theme :dark :font 14}])
    | proves :OtherKindNotTakenError/refusesASetOfKeys
        |~~ A set of keys merges with no map. ~~|
        ~({:theme :dark} | merge #[:lang] !| tag | eq ::map/merge/OtherKindNotTakenError)
    | impl ~(union other);

:map/minus
  |~~ Answers the entries of the map whose keys are not among the keys the
      call gives, under the tags of the map: the keys of a map, or the
      keys a vector or a set names, each a keyword, a number or a string,
      as `get` reads a key alone, a number naming the key of its text.
      `{:name "Ann" :tmp 3} | minus #[:tmp]` answers `{:name "Ann"}`, and
      a key the map lacks changes nothing. A single key is written in a
      set, `minus #[:tmp]`. A set and a vector take out their elements by
      a `minus` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/inter) ::link~(::map/union)
      ::link~(::set/minus)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :keys
        |~~ The keys to leave out: a map, or a vector or a set of keys. ~~|
        /0 #[::map ::vec]
    | throws :KeysKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The keys are a map, or a vector or a set of keys; a single key
            is written in a set, `minus #[:tmp]`.
        ~~|
    | throws :KeysElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ A key `minus` takes out of a map is a keyword, a number or a
            string, as `get` reads a key alone; `:index` names the first
            element of the keys that is none of them, and `:expected` the
            three kinds.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The entries whose keys are not among the keys, under the tags
            of the map.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | proves :leavesTheKeysOfASet
        |~~ The keys a set names leave. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann" :age 30 :tmp 3} | minus #[:tmp] | eq {:name "Ann" :age 30})
    | proves :leavesTheKeysOfAVector
        |~~ A string in a vector names a key to leave. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann" :age 30} | minus ["age"] | eq {:name "Ann"})
    | proves :leavesTheKeysOfAMap
        |~~ The keys of a map leave, whatever their values. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann" :age 30} | minus {:age null} | eq {:name "Ann"})
    | proves :keepsTheMapForAKeyItLacks
        |~~ A key the map lacks changes nothing. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann"} | minus [:phone] | eq {:name "Ann"})
    | proves :answersUnderTheTagOfTheMap
        |~~ A map under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Person{:name "Ann" :tmp 3} | minus [:tmp] | eq ::Person{:name "Ann"})
    | proves :leavesTheKeyOfANumber
        |~~ A number names the key of its text, as `get` reads it. ~~|
        ~({:"2024" 12 :"2025" 15} | minus [2024] | eq {:"2025" 15})
    | proves :KeysKindNotTakenError/refusesASingleKey
        |~~ A key written alone is refused; a set holds it. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann" :tmp 3} | minus "tmp" !| tag | eq ::map/minus/KeysKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :KeysElementKindNotTakenError/refusesABoolean
        |~~ A boolean among the keys is refused under the input at its
            index, with the kinds of a key.
        ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann"} | minus [:tmp true] !| get [~(tag) ~(keys | found | get /1 | tag) ~(keys | index) ~(keys | expected)]
          |  eq [::map/minus/KeysElementKindNotTakenError ::boolean 1 #[::keyword ::number ::string]])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/inter
  |~~ Answers the entries of the map whose keys are among the keys the
      call gives, under the tags of the map: the keys of a map, or the
      keys a vector or a set names, each a keyword, a number or a string,
      as `get` reads a key alone.
      `{:name "Ann" :age 30 :tmp 3} | inter #[:age :name]` answers
      `{:name "Ann" :age 30}`, and keys the map lacks keep none of its
      entries. A set and a vector keep the elements they share by an
      `inter` of their own.
      ::link~(::map/minus) ::link~(::set/inter)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :keys
        |~~ The keys to keep: a map, or a vector or a set of keys. ~~|
        /0 #[::map ::vec]
    | throws :KeysKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The keys are a map, or a vector or a set of keys,
            `inter #[:name]`.
        ~~|
    | throws :KeysElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ A key `inter` keeps of a map is a keyword, a number or a
            string, as `get` reads a key alone; `:index` names the first
            element of the keys that is none of them, and `:expected` the
            three kinds.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The entries whose keys are among the keys, under the tags of
            the map.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | proves :keepsTheKeysOfASet
        |~~ The keys a set names stay. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann" :age 30 :tmp 3} | inter #[:age :name] | eq {:name "Ann" :age 30})
    | proves :keepsTheKeysOfAVector
        |~~ Strings and keywords in a vector name the keys to keep. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann" :age 30 :tmp 3} | inter ["name" :age] | eq {:name "Ann" :age 30})
    | proves :keepsTheKeysOfAMap
        |~~ The keys of a map stay, whatever their values. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann" :age 30} | inter {:age null} | eq {:age 30})
    | proves :keepsNoEntryForKeysTheMapLacks
        |~~ Keys the map lacks keep none of its entries. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann" :age 30} | inter [:phone] | eq {})
    | proves :KeysElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector among the keys is refused under the input at its
            index, with the kinds of a key.
        ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann"} | inter [[:name]] !| get [~(tag) ~(keys | found | get /0 | tag) ~(keys | index) ~(keys | expected)]
          |  eq [::map/inter/KeysElementKindNotTakenError ::vec 0 #[::keyword ::number ::string]])
    | proves :KeysKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ Keys that are no map, vector or set are refused under the
            input.
        ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann"} | inter 5 !| tag | eq ::map/inter/KeysKindNotTakenError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:map/toMap
  |~~ Answers the map itself, so `map ~toMap` turns maps and vectors of
      pairs into maps.
      ::link~(::vec/toMap)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The map itself. ~~| ::map
    | proves :answersItself
        |~~ A map answers itself. ~~|
        ~({:name "Ann"} | toMap | eq {:name "Ann"})
    | impl ::builtin{};

